winebus: send the lights a user chose again, because macOS repaints them.

mgvf-0031 rewrites the colour in a client's own light packets and adds no
packet of its own. With the pad seized that was enough. With the pad shared
(mgvf-0034) it is not.

WHAT WAS MEASURED on 2026-09-15, Mortal Shell 2 under Steam Input, a DualSense
Edge on Bluetooth with SeizeDevice 0 and a red lightbar chosen (hid-122915.log
beside macOS's own log; the two clocks were tied by the disconnect, bluetoothd
12:46:26.620 against the trace's removal, and the reconnect agreed within
0.01 s):

- 12:30:00.37: Steam's light packet rewritten to ff0000, after the release
  (status 0);
- 12:30:00.91: gamecontrollerd "Applying light color ... active application"
  as the game became the front application. The user saw the colour
  unchanged, and no client wrote lights again for 16 minutes;
- after a reconnect, gamecontrollerd painted at 12:46:37.20 and the rewritten
  Steam packet went out at 12:46:39.18, last; the user saw red;
- gamecontrollerd applied a colour again at every later change of front
  application, whoever it was.

Nothing on this side can see macOS's writes, so the lights are re-asserted.

THE CHANGE:

- dualsense_usb.c, pure and tested: dualsense_bt_lights_report and
  dualsense_usb_lights_report build a lights-only packet -- flag1 enables only
  the fields the user chose, those carry the user's values (the player pattern
  with the instant bit), every other byte is zero, both motors included, and
  the Bluetooth form is signed. dualsense_lights_carried says whether a packet
  going to the pad carries a chosen field.
- main.c: dualsense_lights_fixup stamps dualsense_lights_sent when the packet
  it has just rewritten carries the user's lights to a pad on Bluetooth.
  process_hid_report, under ext->cs like every client write, sends the
  lights-only packet whenever DUALSENSE_LIGHTS_REASSERT_MS, 2000, have passed
  since the last packet carrying them. The emulated route sends it as a
  48-byte 0x02 through the same translation as the release.

WHY THESE RULES:

- two seconds was the user's choice: after macOS repaints, its colour can
  show for up to that long. It costs one 78-byte write every two seconds on a
  link measured to drain about 65 a second;
- nothing before the first client packet carrying the lights, which keeps
  mgvf-0031's rule that a title nobody writes lights for gets no packet of
  ours, and keeps the release ahead of any colour;
- the pad's own input is the clock, so a pad that sends nothing is not written
  to, and no thread is added;
- Bluetooth only, the transport the repaint was measured on;
- zero motors, so no rumble is started or stopped: the vibration rewrite finds
  nothing in the packet at any mode or gain, which the host test holds.

NOT CLAIMED: that this has run on a pad yet; its frame cost; that the instant
bit keeps a re-sent pattern from fading in again; that a lightbar macOS paints
between two packets is invisible -- up to two seconds of it is expected.

Host test: dualsense-descriptors/usb_emu_test.c, section (t).

---
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.h
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.h
@@ -517,4 +517,31 @@
 unsigned int dualsense_usb_lights_release_report(unsigned char *out, unsigned int out_size,
                                                  unsigned int len);
 
+/* MGVF mgvf-0035: a pad shared with macOS has its lightbar repainted by macOS
+ * whenever the front application changes (gamecontrollerd, measured on
+ * 2026-09-15: 0.54 s after a rewritten colour of ours, which then stayed
+ * overwritten for the 16 minutes nobody wrote lights again). So once a packet
+ * carrying the user's lights has gone to a pad on Bluetooth, the lights go to
+ * it again whenever none has gone for this long. Two seconds was the user's
+ * choice: macOS's colour can show for up to that long after it repaints, for
+ * one 78-byte write every two seconds on a link that drains about 65 a second. */
+#define DUALSENSE_LIGHTS_REASSERT_MS 2000
+
+/* The lights-only packet: flag1 enables only the fields the user chose, those
+ * fields carry the user's values -- the player byte with the instant bit --
+ * and every other byte is zero, both motors included. Bluetooth: a signed
+ * 78-byte 0x31 with the header 31 00 10. USB: a 0x02 of len bytes
+ * (DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_LIGHTS_MIN..64) for the emulated route, which
+ * translates and signs it itself. Each returns the bytes written, or 0 --
+ * also when nothing is chosen. */
+unsigned int dualsense_bt_lights_report(unsigned char *out, unsigned int out_size,
+                                        const struct dualsense_lights *lights, dualsense_crc32_fn crc32);
+unsigned int dualsense_usb_lights_report(unsigned char *out, unsigned int out_size,
+                                         const struct dualsense_lights *lights, unsigned int len);
+
+/* Whether a packet carries a field the user chose: a 78-byte 0x31 or a
+ * 48..64-byte 0x02 whose flag1 enables the lightbar while a colour is chosen,
+ * or the player LEDs while a pattern is. */
+int dualsense_lights_carried(const unsigned char *report, unsigned int len, const struct dualsense_lights *lights);
+
 #endif /* __WINEBUS_DUALSENSE_USB_H */
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.c
@@ -874,3 +874,74 @@
     out[DUALSENSE_USB_COMMON + DUALSENSE_COMMON_LIGHTBAR_SETUP] = DUALSENSE_LIGHTBAR_SETUP_LIGHT_OUT;
     return len;
 }
+
+/* MGVF mgvf-0035: the lights a user chose, as a packet of our own.
+ *
+ * Only the fields the user chose are enabled and set; flag0, flag2 and both
+ * motors stay zero, so mgvf-0009's rewrite finds nothing in it and no rumble is
+ * started or stopped by it. The player byte carries the instant bit, so that a
+ * pattern sent again every few seconds does not replay its fade -- whether the
+ * pad would replay it without the bit is not measured. */
+static void dualsense_lights_set(unsigned char *common, const struct dualsense_lights *lights)
+{
+    if (lights->bar)
+    {
+        common[DUALSENSE_COMMON_FLAG1] |= DUALSENSE_FLAG1_LIGHTBAR;
+        memcpy(common + DUALSENSE_COMMON_LIGHTBAR, lights->rgb, 3);
+    }
+    if (lights->player)
+    {
+        common[DUALSENSE_COMMON_FLAG1] |= DUALSENSE_FLAG1_PLAYER_LEDS;
+        common[DUALSENSE_COMMON_PLAYER_LEDS] = DUALSENSE_PLAYER_LEDS_INSTANT | lights->pattern;
+    }
+}
+
+unsigned int dualsense_bt_lights_report(unsigned char *out, unsigned int out_size,
+                                        const struct dualsense_lights *lights, dualsense_crc32_fn crc32)
+{
+    static const unsigned char seed = DUALSENSE_CRC_SEED_OUTPUT;
+    unsigned int crc;
+
+    if (out_size < DUALSENSE_BT_REPORT_LEN || !dualsense_lights_wanted(lights)) return 0;
+    memset(out, 0, DUALSENSE_BT_REPORT_LEN);
+    out[0] = DUALSENSE_BT_REPORT_ID;
+    out[1] = 0x00;
+    out[2] = 0x10;
+    dualsense_lights_set(out + DUALSENSE_BT_COMMON, lights);
+
+    crc = crc32(0, &seed, 1);
+    crc = crc32(crc, out, DUALSENSE_BT_REPORT_LEN - DUALSENSE_CRC_TAIL);
+    dualsense_store_crc(out + DUALSENSE_BT_REPORT_LEN - DUALSENSE_CRC_TAIL, crc);
+    return DUALSENSE_BT_REPORT_LEN;
+}
+
+unsigned int dualsense_usb_lights_report(unsigned char *out, unsigned int out_size,
+                                         const struct dualsense_lights *lights, unsigned int len)
+{
+    if (len < DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_LIGHTS_MIN || len > DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_MAX || out_size < len) return 0;
+    if (!dualsense_lights_wanted(lights)) return 0;
+    memset(out, 0, len);
+    out[0] = DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_ID;
+    dualsense_lights_set(out + DUALSENSE_USB_COMMON, lights);
+    return len;
+}
+
+/* Whether a packet going to the pad carries a field the user chose: a 78-byte
+ * 0x31 or a 48..64-byte 0x02 whose flag1 enables the lightbar while a colour is
+ * chosen, or the player LEDs while a pattern is. Asked AFTER the rewrite, so
+ * such a packet carries the user's values and not the client's. */
+int dualsense_lights_carried(const unsigned char *report, unsigned int len, const struct dualsense_lights *lights)
+{
+    unsigned char flag1, wanted = 0;
+
+    if (lights->bar) wanted |= DUALSENSE_FLAG1_LIGHTBAR;
+    if (lights->player) wanted |= DUALSENSE_FLAG1_PLAYER_LEDS;
+    if (len == DUALSENSE_BT_REPORT_LEN && report[0] == DUALSENSE_BT_REPORT_ID)
+        flag1 = report[DUALSENSE_BT_COMMON + DUALSENSE_COMMON_FLAG1];
+    else if (len >= DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_LIGHTS_MIN && len <= DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_MAX
+             && report[0] == DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_ID)
+        flag1 = report[DUALSENSE_USB_COMMON + DUALSENSE_COMMON_FLAG1];
+    else
+        return 0;
+    return !!(flag1 & wanted);
+}
